From: Project Arctic
Subject: win32u: video memory is managed as Windows does: the program in front gets it.

A browser open for hours held 1.4 GB of a 2 GB GeForce, and a game started
next got 621 MB of it: its textures went over the bus and it ran at half
its frame rate, bound by the GPU. Windows (VidMm) pages out the video
memory of the processes behind the foreground one once that one runs short
and pages it back in later. Linux drivers do not page the video memory of a
Vulkan program (NVIDIA has no VK_EXT_pageable_device_local_memory nor
memory priorities; amdgpu and xe page by recent use only). Now win32u does,
on any discrete GPU:

- Claims. The process that owns the foreground window, when a device-local
  heap is nearly full for it (less than a sixteenth of the heap left in its
  budget) or an allocation of its own finds no room, claims video memory in
  a page every process shares (/dev/shm/arctic-vram) and renews the claim
  on each present and budget query while it stays in front; a claim not
  renewed for two seconds is over. A program in front that fits takes
  nothing from anyone.
- Budgets. Behind a claim, a process sees a share of the heap as its budget
  (VK_EXT_memory_budget: a sixteenth, at least 64 MB; DXGI's budget and its
  change events are built on it in DXVK and vkd3d), and a device-local
  allocation past that share goes to system memory the way VRAM that is
  full does (0135). vkGetPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties2 goes through
  win32u for it.
- Paging. In a 64-bit process, images of 64 KB and more and buffers of 1 MB
  and more are created with SPARSE_BINDING when that changes neither the
  memory types they accept nor anything in their chain, and win32u binds
  them with vkQueueBindSparse on a queue it adds to the device; their GPU
  addresses stay when the memory behind them moves (no cost measured on a
  GTX 1050: blits and clears equal). A thread per device moves the memory
  objects of a process behind a claim to system memory, one allocation per
  resource, copying their bytes on the GPU (render targets and depth
  buffers with fast clears verified bit for bit both ways) and binding them
  there, down to its share; they come back to VRAM when there is room and
  no claim. The program's submissions wait meanwhile. A moved memory object
  is a spilled one: what is bound to it later gets memory of its own.
- Only memory whose every use win32u sees moves: something bound to it the
  ordinary way, sharing (import, export), host-visible, lazily allocated,
  protected or capture-replay memory stays; the thunks hand memory to the
  driver through pinned_host_memory, which pins it (and brings it back
  first): the program's own sparse binds, video sessions, priorities,
  names. vkDestroyImage and vkDestroyBuffer go through win32u.

Integrated GPUs and WoW64 processes are left alone.
C:\ProgramData\Arctic\no-vidmm turns paging off for programs started
after. Log: "vulkan: process ... in front is short of video memory", "...
MB of video memory to system memory, ... MB back".
